Super keyword is  used to immediately call the 
parent class constructor using super().

why super() is used to call the parent class constructor?
    --> When we create an object of child class, 
        the constructor of parent class is called first and then the constructor of child class is called.
    --> If we don't explicitly call the parent class constructor using super() in child class constructor,
        then the default constructor of parent class will be called automatically.
    --> If we want to call a parameterized constructor of parent class, 
        then we have to explicitly call it using super() with appropriate arguments.

Rules for using super() in child class constructor:
------------------------------------------------------------
    1) super() should be the first statement in child class constructor.
    2) If we don't call super() explicitly, then java automatically inserts a call to no-argument constructor of parent class.
    3) We can call only one constructor of parent class using super() in child class constructor.

Special Purpose of using super() in child class constructor:
------------------------------------------------------------
1) to call the parent class constructor from child class constructor.
2) used as a keyword to access the parent class methods and variables from child class.

super.methodname();
super.var_name;

